    ":question/id": "19a2dc0a-21a4-5b89-85c8-47422a295e17",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-974",
    ":question/problem": "Given that $\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {3}^{x}=5,$ find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {3}^{x} & =5 \\\\ {3}^{x} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take ${log}_{3}$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & =2 \\\\ {log}_{3}\u2061({3}^{x}) & ={log}_{3}\u2061(2) \\\\ x & ={log}_{3}\u2061(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(2)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(3)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx\u22480.631\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."
